  <h1>Many-Body Perturbation Theory and Quasiparticles</h1>
  <p class="lede">Many-body perturbation theory describes electron addition and removal through Green functions and self-energies, then builds neutral electron–hole excitations from screened interactions.</p>
  <p>This page separates charged quasiparticle energies from neutral optical excitations. It also separates the formal approximation from the production workflow: “GW” and “BSE” denote families of choices whose numerical realization, self-consistency, screening, and convergence must be declared.</p>

  <h2>The Dyson equation replaces a static potential by an energy-dependent self-energy</h2>
  <p>The interacting one-particle Green function satisfies</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Dyson equation G inverse equals G zero inverse minus Sigma">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^&#123;-1&#125;(\omega)=G_0^&#123;-1&#125;(\omega)-\Sigma(\omega).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The self-energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Sigma"><semantics><mi>Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is generally nonlocal, energy-dependent, complex, and non-Hermitian away from isolated poles. Its real part shifts excitation energies; its imaginary part is related to finite lifetimes and spectral broadening.</p>
  <p>A local static exchange–correlation potential and a many-body self-energy are not equivalent objects, even when a perturbative correction starts from Kohn–Sham orbitals.</p>

  <h2>Screening replaces the bare Coulomb interaction by a medium-dependent interaction</h2>
  <p>The screened interaction obeys a Dyson-like relation</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="W equals v plus v P W">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>v</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=v+vPW=(1-vP)^&#123;-1&#125;v.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Here <math class="math-inline" aria-label="P"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible polarizability. Screening depends on frequency, wavevector, dimensionality, dielectric environment, band structure, occupation, and approximation.</p>
  <p>Supercell vacuum does not automatically eliminate image screening in low-dimensional systems. Coulomb truncation, dielectric matrix size, and long-wavelength treatment can be decisive.</p>

  <h2>The GW approximation retains a first screened-interaction self-energy</h2>
  <p>The defining approximation is commonly written</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Sigma GW equals i G W">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>Σ</mi><mi>GW</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_&#123;GW&#125;(1,2)=iG(1,2)W(1,2^+).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Vertex corrections are omitted at this level. The notation hides choices about the starting Green function, polarizability, frequency treatment, self-consistency, spin, relativistic effects, and core–valence partition.</p>

  <h2>Quasiparticle energies solve an energy-dependent equation</h2>
  <p>For a Kohn–Sham starting orbital <math class="math-inline" aria-label="phi n"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_n</annotation></semantics></math>, a real-energy diagonal linearization around the reference eigenvalue is often written</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="quasiparticle energy correction equation">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Re</mi><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>Σ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>xc</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_n=\varepsilon_n+Z_n\,\mathrm&#123;Re&#125;\langle\phi_n|\Sigma(\varepsilon_n)-v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;|\phi_n\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The corresponding renormalization factor is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Z n equals one minus frequency derivative of real self energy matrix element inverse">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <msup>
            <mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo>
              <msub>
                <mrow><mfrac><mi>∂</mi><mrow><mi>∂</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Re</mi><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>Σ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow>
                <mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow>
              </msub>
            <mo>]</mo></mrow>
            <mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
          </msup><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_n=[1-\left.\partial_\omega\mathrm&#123;Re&#125;\langle\phi_n|\Sigma(\omega)|\phi_n\rangle\right|_&#123;\omega=\varepsilon_n&#125;]^&#123;-1&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The subtraction of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="v x c"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>xc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;xc&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is specific to this starting point; another reference requires subtraction of its own mean-field operator. The formula targets the real quasiparticle energy. A complex pole or spectral-function calculation retains the imaginary self-energy and lifetime information rather than discarding it. Linearization, diagonal approximations, and neglect of off-diagonal mixing can fail near degeneracies, band inversions, strong hybridization, or satellite structures.</p>

  <h2>GW denotes a family, not one uniquely defined calculation</h2>
  <dl class="paired-definitions">
    <dt><math class="math-inline" aria-label="G zero W zero"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_0W_0</annotation></semantics></math></dt>
    <dd>One-shot correction from a fixed mean-field starting point. Results can depend strongly on that starting point.</dd>
    <dt>Eigenvalue self-consistency</dt>
    <dd>Updates selected quasiparticle energies while retaining some orbitals or screening approximations.</dd>
    <dt>Quasiparticle self-consistency</dt>
    <dd>Constructs an updated effective one-particle Hamiltonian from the self-energy under a declared mapping.</dd>
    <dt>Fully self-consistent GW</dt>
    <dd>Iterates the Green function and screened interaction, changing conservation properties, spectra, and cost.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>These variants do not form a universal accuracy ladder. Vertex corrections, cancellation of errors, starting-point quality, and the target observable can reverse simple expectations.</p>

  <h2>Neutral excitations require an electron–hole equation</h2>
  <p>After quasiparticle energies and screening are constructed, a static-kernel resonant or Tamm–Dancoff form of the Bethe–Salpeter equation can be written as an effective electron–hole eigenproblem,</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="BSE electron hole eigenvalue equation">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo></mo></munderover><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_&#123;v'c'&#125;H_&#123;vc,v'c'&#125;A_&#123;v'c'&#125;^S=\Omega_S A_&#123;vc&#125;^S.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The basis labels occupied–empty transitions. The kernel includes direct screened attraction and exchange-like terms under the chosen approximation. Its eigenvalues describe neutral excitations; they are not quasiparticle addition/removal energies. The full BSE can couple resonant and antiresonant sectors, so the simple eigenproblem above must not be presented as formulation-independent.</p>

  <h2>Optical gaps, fundamental gaps, and exciton binding are distinct</h2>
  <p>The fundamental gap is a charged excitation difference. For exciton <math class="math-inline" aria-label="S"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> at total crystal momentum <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;Q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, binding is defined relative to the free electron–hole continuum threshold in the same sector:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="exciton binding at Q equals continuum threshold at Q minus exciton energy at Q">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">Q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>cont</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">Q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">Q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;b,S&#125;(\mathbf&#123;Q&#125;)=E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;cont&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;Q&#125;)-\Omega_S(\mathbf&#123;Q&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Only in a matching direct sector may the continuum threshold be identified with the chosen quasiparticle gap. The lowest bright optical onset may differ because of dark or indirect states, spin selection, phonon assistance, finite temperature, or the dielectric environment. The compared quantities must come from compatible geometries, screening models, spin/SOC settings, and numerical convergence levels.</p>

  <h2>Production convergence is multi-dimensional</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Mean-field starting point, functional, pseudopotential or all-electron treatment, and spin/SOC settings.</li>
    <li>Number and energy range of empty states, dielectric cutoff, response basis, frequency grid or contour model.</li>
    <li>k-point sampling, q-point transfer, Coulomb singularity treatment, vacuum, truncation, and finite-size effects.</li>
    <li>Self-energy linearization, off-diagonal terms, self-consistency variant, and analytic continuation.</li>
    <li>BSE transition subspace, screening, exchange, Tamm–Dancoff choice, broadening, and oscillator-strength convergence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Convergence of the quasiparticle gap does not establish convergence of absolute band edges, effective masses, lifetimes, satellite weights, exciton energies, or spectra.</p>

  <h2>Validation requires more than agreement with one experimental number</h2>
  <p>Useful checks include sum rules, limiting cases, symmetry and degeneracy, comparison among frequency treatments, basis and empty-state extrapolation, dimensional Coulomb tests, consistency between charged and neutral gaps, and comparison with well-characterized reference systems.</p>
  <p>Agreement with experiment can contain cancellation among geometry, electron–phonon renormalization, temperature, disorder, substrate screening, relativistic effects, and many-body approximation errors. The comparison conditions must be aligned.</p>

