  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://journals.aps.org/pr/abstract/10.1103/PhysRev.139.A796">Lars Hedin, “New Method for Calculating the One-Particle Green's Function”</a> is the primary source for the screened-interaction hierarchy underlying GW. It is a research paper, not a first introduction.</p>
  <p><a href="https://epw2024.oden.utexas.edu/74-schedule">University of Texas at Austin, <cite>2024 School on Electron-Phonon Physics, Many-Body Perturbation Theory, and Computational Workflows</cite></a> provides a traceable graduate route from theory lectures to implementation material. The public schedule links recorded lectures and slides for GW and the Bethe–Salpeter equation, GW perturbation theory, and related excited-state topics, followed by BerkeleyGW tutorials and a hackathon on quasiparticle band structures and excitons. It is valuable as a lecture-to-workflow bridge, but the 2024 software versions, tutorial files, examples, and computing environment are time-specific; reproducing them does not supply universal convergence values or independent validation.</p>
  <p><a href="https://berkeleygw.org/documentation/tutorial/">BerkeleyGW Tutorial</a> connects mean-field input, dielectric screening, GW self-energy, BSE kernels, and absorption calculations. It is a software-specific workflow bridge and does not supply universal convergence values.</p>
  <p><a href="https://yambo-code.org/tutorials/index.php">The Yambo Project tutorials</a> provide a second open implementation route through GW, spectral functions, TDDFT, and Bethe–Salpeter optical calculations, with visible topic-specific tutorial structure. Use them after mean-field and Green-function foundations are secure: the project warns that some tutorials use older Yambo versions and reference databases, scripts, and input variables can require adaptation, so a reproduced walkthrough is not a convergence or cross-code validation result.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page does not present GW as a universal correction to every DFT result, does not identify BSE excitons with quasiparticle poles, and does not treat one-shot agreement with experiment as independent validation. The variant, starting point, screening, dimensional treatment, numerical limits, and target observable must be stated.</p>

  <aside class="review-note">
    <p><strong>Review note.</strong> The page remains separate from Linear Response because quasiparticle self-energy and electron–hole calculations have distinct objects, workflows, and convergence boundaries. Official APS, BerkeleyGW, Yambo, and UT Austin 2024 school destinations were reviewed through 4 August 2026; school and software examples remain version-specific learning material rather than universal validation.</p>
  </aside>
</BaseLayout>
